{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`verify.enable_code_review` (default False — src/rebar/_config_schema.py:191, parsed in src/rebar/_config_sections.py:26) is wired as an ENABLEMENT switch for the whole code-review capability, not an ENFORCEMENT switch for the gate. `gate_dispatch.code_review_enabled()` (src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py:273) is consulted at the top of `rebar.llm.code_review.shim.review_code()` (src/rebar/llm/code_review/shim.py:168), and when the flag is off a MANUAL, explicit invocation — the CLI `rebar review-code`, the library `review_code()`, the MCP `review_code` tool — does zero work and returns the inert `_disabled_review_result()` (\"code-review capability disabled (verify.enable_code_review is off).\", empty findings, UNPINNED provenance).\n\nWhy this is wrong:\n- An explicit manual invocation IS the operator's statement of intent. Gating it on a config var conflates two different questions: \"should code review run when asked?\" (always yes — the user asked) and \"should code review be ENFORCED as a blocking gate in automated flows?\" (the thing a config knob should decide).\n- AGENTS.md and `rebar explain review` tell authors to \"preview the review locally with `rebar review-code` before you push\". On any checkout that has not flipped `verify.enable_code_review`, that documented flow silently returns an empty PASS-looking verdict — a trap: the author believes their change was reviewed clean when nothing ran.\n- The workaround inside rebar itself proves the design smell: the review-bot adapter must FORCE-ENABLE the capability to do its job (src/rebar/review_bot/adapter.py:16, `request.enabled` override at gate_dispatch.py:373).\n- Sibling gates already model this correctly: the criteria-registry posture distinguishes per-criterion `blocking_enabled` (src/rebar/llm/criteria/model.py:62) — i.e., enforcement — from the operation being invocable at all.\n\n## Approach (direction, refine at claim time)\nRepurpose the config axis from enablement to enforcement:\n- A manual/explicit `review-code` invocation (CLI, library, MCP) always runs the four-pass gate — no config precondition beyond a working LLM config.\n- The config knob (renamed or reinterpreted, e.g. `verify.enforce_code_review` / gate-posture semantics) decides only whether automated flows treat code-review as a required/blocking gate (voter posture, workflow dispatch), preserving today's off-by-default ENFORCEMENT.\n- Keep the inert-empty-result shape for automated dispatch paths when enforcement is off, so existing pipelines that probe the gate cheaply do not start paying LLM costs.\n- Decide the compatibility story for the existing `verify.enable_code_review` key explicitly (tombstone-warn or alias), matching the deprecation conventions used for prior config-key removals.\n\n## File impact\n- src/rebar/llm/code_review/shim.py — drop the enabled() precondition from the manual entrypoint\n- src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py — split enforcement posture from invocability\n- src/rebar/_config_schema.py — enforcement-semantics key (+ compat handling for enable_code_review)\n- src/rebar/_config_sections.py — parse the new/renamed key\n- src/rebar/review_bot/adapter.py — remove the force-enable workaround\n- docs/config.md — document enforcement semantics\n\n## Scope narrowed by operator ruling (2026-08-21, recorded in comments)\n\"review-code should not require a config key to be active. It should operate comparably to plan review: a config key controls whether it is REQUIRED, not whether it is AVAILABLE. Making it required is out of scope for this bug; dropping the config key gating availability is approved.\" The enforcement-repurposing items below are therefore struck from this bug's scope; only the availability-gate removal lands here (Gerrit change 2010, commit a1a6a151bc).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `rebar review-code` on a checkout with no code-review config key set runs the four-pass gate and returns real findings (not the inert \"capability disabled\" result).\n- [x] The library `review_code()` and MCP `review_code` tool behave identically to the CLI in that respect.\n- [x] A config knob still exists that controls whether AUTOMATED dispatch runs the code-review gate, default off, and automated dispatch honors it: `verify.enable_code_review` (src/rebar/_config_schema.py) is consulted by `_code_review_preflight` when a `CodeReviewRequest` leaves `enabled=None` (src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py), pinned by tests/unit/test_code_review_ws4.py::test_produce_verdict_disabled_is_inert_and_makes_zero_llm_calls and tests/unit/test_llm_failure_matrix.py::test_disabled_code_review_never_invokes_runner. (Per the operator ruling, REPURPOSING it into a blocking-enforcement knob is out of scope for this bug.)\n- [x] The review-bot adapter (src/rebar/review_bot/adapter.py) keeps its explicit `enabled=True` — per the operator ruling and the 2026-08-17 escalation comment, receiver deployment is its enforcement control — and its module docstring now documents that this is the same intent-is-authoritative semantics as the explicit surface, not a workaround.\n- [x] The `verify.enable_code_review` key is neither renamed nor deprecated (struck per the operator ruling — no compatibility disposition is needed): it keeps its current meaning for `enabled=None` dispatch callers, documented at its declaration in src/rebar/_config_schema.py and in `code_review_enabled`'s docstring (src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py).\n- [x] The `rebar explain review` guide and llm-framework/config docs describe the availability semantics (explicit invocation always runs; the key gates only `enabled=None` dispatch). docs/config.md carries no entry for this key; the generated docs/config-reference.md row remains accurate unchanged."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787337964884103001,"uuid":"4658c226-98ac-452a-b662-a06547a76124"}